Description: CVE-2026-112319 - Enforce XDR decode size limits
 Add GSSRPC_MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE, GSSRPC_MAX_ELEMENT_SIZE and
 GSSRPC_MAX_ARRAY_COUNT to <gssrpc/xdr.h> and use them in place of unbounded
 (~0 / -1) limits in xdr_bytes(), xdr_array() and xdr_string() calls in the
 kadmin RPC and RPCSEC_GSS decoding paths.  This prevents a remote client
 from forcing very large up-front allocations (memory-exhaustion denial of
 service in kadmind).  xdr_gprincs_ret() is intentionally left unbounded as
 in upstream.  Includes the upstream follow-up that parenthesizes the
 size check in xdr_nullstring() to avoid a compiler warning.
 Backported to krb5 1.20.1 (array/bytes call sites differ from upstream).
 .
 Rebased on top of the CVE-2026-112315 and CVE-2026-112316 patches of this
 hook: hunks in lib/rpc/authgss_prot.c (xdr_rpc_gss_unwrap_data) and
 lib/kadm5/kadm_rpc_xdr.c (xdr_krb5_tl_data) were adjusted only in their
 context lines so the series applies in CVE-ID order; the changes are
 identical to the Jira patch.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/krb5/krb5/commit/6946435bc3e66b1cccef0c41105bbd1e7f8c1a92
 and https://github.com/krb5/krb5/commit/bdf3a4dc3c0b76492a5483e40b2112c801495795
Author: Greg Hudson <ghudson@mit.edu>
Last-Update: 2026-11-17
